1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,687
recursive dflash two.

2
00:00:01,687 --> 00:00:05,976
How fast can speculative decoding go, and does stacking drafters help?

3
00:00:05,976 --> 00:00:11,735
This is a head to head benchmark of four drafters, plus a tree verification upgrade that needs

4
00:00:11,735 --> 00:00:12,593
no retraining.

5
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:17,499
A large language model normally produces one token per forward pass.

6
00:00:17,499 --> 00:00:22,960
Speculative decoding lets a small drafter guess a block of tokens, and the big model checks the

7
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:24,627
whole block in a single pass.

8
00:00:24,627 --> 00:00:26,754
Every accepted token is a pass saved.

9
00:00:26,754 --> 00:00:32,100
The questions are: which drafter wins, and can we verify more than a single chain of guesses?

10
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:35,283
A chain verifies one guessed sequence.

11
00:00:35,283 --> 00:00:40,110
DSpine scores sixteen candidates for each position, conditioned on the previous one.

12
00:00:40,110 --> 00:00:45,396
We use those exact conditional scores to build a tree of up to forty eight nodes, verify the

13
00:00:45,396 --> 00:00:50,452
whole tree in one pass with a tree attention mask, and accept the longest matching path.

14
00:00:51,450 --> 00:00:58,845
On Qwen3 eight B, one A100, batch one, greedy decoding, averaged over five datasets.

15
00:00:58,845 --> 00:01:03,423
EAGLE three reaches 2.03 times autoregressive speed.

16
00:01:03,423 --> 00:01:05,184
DFlash reaches 4.50.

17
00:01:05,184 --> 00:01:06,328
DSpine, 5.75.

18
00:01:06,328 --> 00:01:11,083
DSpine with the same weights and a longer block, 6.17.

19
00:01:11,083 --> 00:01:14,516
And with tree verification, 7.20 times.

20
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:22,495
That is 7.20 times autoregressive decoding, 25 percent more than DSpine's own chain, with

21
00:01:22,495 --> 00:01:24,454
exactly the same weights.

22
00:01:24,454 --> 00:01:31,429
Mean acceptance length rises from 7.99 to 11.29 tokens per round, at about 652 tokens per

23
00:01:31,429 --> 00:01:31,977
second.

24
00:01:32,980 --> 00:01:39,840
Across temperatures and concurrency, DSpine beats DFlash and EAGLE three in every cell we ran.

25
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:45,094
At concurrency eight, DSpine reaches 4.11 times against 3.40 for DFlash.

26
00:01:45,094 --> 00:01:50,056
With sampling at temperature one and batch one, DSpine reaches 4.85.

27
00:01:51,060 --> 00:01:56,560
Longer blocks help at batch one, but at concurrency eight verification is no longer free and

28
00:01:56,560 --> 00:01:57,935
the longer blocks lose.

29
00:01:57,935 --> 00:01:59,968
The tree gain exists at batch one.

30
00:02:00,970 --> 00:02:02,501
How to read these numbers.

31
00:02:02,501 --> 00:02:07,858
Speedups are same cell ratios to autoregressive decoding, with noise of one to two percent.

32
00:02:07,858 --> 00:02:10,154
The tree gain exists at batch one only.

33
00:02:10,154 --> 00:02:14,629
Our EAGLE three baseline is a three step chain, which is the weaker variant.

34
00:02:14,629 --> 00:02:20,163
And the eight B runs use forty prompts per dataset; LiveCodeBench and Arena Hard were not run.

35
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:26,453
So: DSpine is the best single drafter we measured, tree verification adds 25 percent with no

36
00:02:26,453 --> 00:02:31,747
retraining, and our longer block training and recursive second pass experiments did not beat

37
00:02:31,747 --> 00:02:32,265
the tree.

38
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:37,512
The raw results, scripts, patches and write ups are all in the repo.

